            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house had an excessive amount of air leakage, and there are some key areas that we identified. The majority of the air leakage is associated with the breach in the vapour barrier &amp; insulation in the cathedral ceiling above the laundry area. The 2nd major leakage area is around the small access door to the furnace &amp; tank room.



Other air leakage locations are associated with the 5" furnace chimney, and some minor leakage around the doors (replace weather-stripping &amp; door sweeps on a regular basis). If you complete the heating &amp; hot water system upgrades outlined within this report, the current furnace chimney will be eliminated.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

Based on the two large air leakage locations identified above, I think you could achieve a 30% improvement and potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 775&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;32.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 700 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 construction with R20 batt insulation, including the wall adjoining the garage. In conjunction with a future exterior renovation of the house, I would recommend you add a minimum 2" (R10) of insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an additional R10 insulation to 100% of the exterior walls of your home, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 435&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;18.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;b&gt;The upgrade recommendation for your domestic hot water is integrated into the boiler upgrade recommendation above.&lt;/b&gt;</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Home Heating &amp; Hot Water + other Zone Heating&lt;/b&gt;



HEATING



Your house is primarily heated with an oil-fired furnace (venting up a 5" chimney) that should be about 84% efficient. This furnace has no dedicated combustion air supply pipe and the fresh air supply connected to the return air plenum has been disconnected. The thermostat for the furnace has been relocated to the furnace &amp; tank room and is set quite low as you want to primarily heat the upper floors with your woodstove.



The furnace ductwork serves the tank room, the upper living areas &amp; the dog-grooming work area, so 100% of your house is interconnected via this ductwork. The heat for the dog-grooming area is supplemented with an oil-fired Toyotomi Laser 30 stove.



HOT WATER



Your house has a 178 litre electric hot water tank with an R5 insulation blanket around it. Although electricity in Yellowknife (primarily a hydro generation community) is relatively GHG free, having an electric hot water tank can be very expensive.



AN INTEGRATION OPPORTUNITY



During the assessment we talked about an opportunity for you to install an ENERGY STAR® direct vent, condensing propane boiler (95% AFUE), eliminating the current chimney and zoned to accomplish many objectives:



1. Incorporate one zone to heat your house via a fan coil with an integrated heat exchanger mounted where the current oil furnace exist. You could have the fan of this unit wired to circulate the warm air from the fireplace. A fresh air supply duct can be incorporated (with a damper to control the amount of fresh air). The thermostat for this zone could be located on the main floor in either the right or left side of the house (likely on the wall that adjoins the 2 sides.



2. A zone can be used to heat your domestic hot water, still including an integrated water storage tank to prevent short cycling of the boiler.



3. Another zone could be used (via a few small hot water radiators) to heat your dog-grooming business, with its' own thermostat.



4. There is always the opportunity to add another small zone to serve just the lower tank room, with it's own thermostat and set to a lower temp.



5. Although you can still choose to use your woodstove as much as you like, you would have a system that would provide good backup if you were away for a few weeks or longer.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with an ENERGY STAR® direct vent, condensing propane boiler (95% AFUE), and using this boiler to also heat your domestic hot water (with an integrated storage tank), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 689&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;34.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. With the current price of oil being so low compared to the historical price difference between oil &amp; propane - the $689 of estimated savings is much less than what it has been. What's the future price of oil &amp; propane - my crystal ball is fuzzy ! 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Woodstove&lt;/b&gt;



I modeled the anticipated energy savings (based on your current oil furnace) if you were to use your current woodstove for 50% of the heating load of your house, based upon:

1. The Yellowknife delivered wood rate of about $340 per cord

2. A nominal $100 per cord rate for those who self harvest



You should be able to have your mechanical contractor arrange a high wall return air duct so you can use your furnace fan to recirculate the heat from your woodstove around the house. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your woodstove to offset 50% of the heating load of your house, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 325&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs (at the YK delivered cost of wood). If you harvest your owjn wood, the estimated annual savings increase to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,400&lt;/b&gt;.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Combustion Air&lt;/b&gt;



Your current oil furnace does not have a dedicated combustion air supply, so it relies on air leakage coming into that lower tank room (primarily from around the poorly fitted access door). The fresh air supply pipe has been disconnected. If you keep this furnace (and you complete extensive air sealing work), I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your furnace are being satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the furnace is operating.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the left (south facing) roof of your house, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1005&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;12.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year in a hydro community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood is an internal recirculation model, so the exhaust fans include 2 bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.6 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home from your combustion appliances (furnace and woodstove).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



